'''Dispersed knowledge''' describes knowledge distributed among different people or institutions rather than held in full by any one decision-maker. People often have local knowledge of conditions, needs and opportunities that others do not possess.

== Knowledge problem ==
Hayek's 1945 essay distinguishes general scientific knowledge from particular circumstances of time and place. Local observations can be incomplete, transient or contradictory. Central collection faces communication and interpretation costs; decentralized decisions also face externalities, incompatible incentives and missing information. The essay is a theoretical argument, not a demonstration that any particular market dominates every alternative.

== Distributed decision model ==
Let actor i observe local signal x_i about state s and choose action a_i under communication constraints. A centralized decision rule might observe all x_i; a decentralized rule conditions a_i on x_i and permitted messages. Compare expected joint loss under these information structures, including transmission cost and delays. The centralized benchmark itself requires assumptions about truthful reports, correct aggregation and computational feasibility. Prices can summarize scarcity without revealing every underlying observation; they may also omit environmental or other unpriced costs.

== Evidence and applications ==
In a controlled allocation experiment, vary the distribution of information and communication rules while holding resource constraints constant. Measure welfare or task success, decision delay and distribution of errors. Include mechanisms such as deliberation and shared records as comparators. Dispersed knowledge concerns the distribution of relevant knowledge; distributed cognition additionally examines coordination of cognitive processes across people and artifacts. Neither entails that every participant is well informed.

